526	\subsubsection*{Standard rescues fail; the raw-input principle generalizes}
527	
528	If the input distribution is the bottleneck, two predictions follow: optimization-side rescues should not recover the gap, and the raw-input fix should transfer beyond RR-MoA's specific router. We test each in turn.
529	
530	\textbf{Rescue-baseline sweep.} The mechanism predicts that interventions targeting optimization will fail because the router's input distribution is the bottleneck, not its objective. We test this directly by sweeping five standard MoE rescue families (Switch load-balance~\citep{fedus2022switch}, ST-MoE z-loss~\citep{zoph2022stmoe}, entropy regularization, ReMoE ReLU routing~\citep{wang2025remoe}, expert-choice routing~\citep{zhou2022expertchoice}) over $12$ configurations $\times$ 6 datasets $\times$ 2 freeze levels $\times$ 5 seeds $= 720$ run-cells (Table~\ref{tab:rescue}, Appendix~\ref{app:rescue_details}). The best rescue (z-loss $c_z{=}0.01$) reduces MSE by only $10.9\%$, $2.7{\times}$ worse than RR-MoA; excessive balancing ($\alpha{=}10.0$) hurts by $+67.7\%$. Consistent with Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}(ii): when routing information is structurally absent, no tested rescue compensates.
531	
532	\textbf{The principle is architecture-agnostic.} Three router-level interventions cross-cut the design space: each varies a different axis (router architecture, router input, expert architecture) while holding the others fixed, so a common improvement isolates the raw-input axis as causal. \textbf{SR-MoA} (Figure~\ref{fig:srmoa_arch}; cf.~\citealp{lv2025aoe,liu2026routingfree,cheng2025ermoe}) drops the external router for per-expert sigmoid gates $\sigma_k(\mathbf{X}_\mathrm{raw})$ and \emph{outperforms} RR-MoA by $13$--$42\%$ on all 6 datasets ($252$ runs, six backbones; Table~\ref{tab:self_routed}), also replicating the Frozen Paradox ($4/6$). Gating the same architecture on hidden states degrades by $+87$--$768\%$ ($p{=}0.016$); on Timer-XL the same comparison yields only $+4$--$9\%$, $15$--$100{\times}$ smaller. \textbf{AdaMix-Raw} (Appendix~\ref{app:adamix_details}) swaps AdaMix's router input only and recovers entropy to $1.41$--$1.57$ and MSE by $41$--$88\%$ on all 12 cells. \textbf{Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}} (Appendix~\ref{app:gate_pathology}, Figure~\ref{fig:residual_ia_arch}) extends the principle to the expert level (shared NLinear raw branch + gated backbone residual). Together with the RevIN ablation (Table~\ref{tab:adamix}), the MOMENT-vs-Timer-XL contrast (Fig.~\ref{fig:causal_contrast}), the BatchNorm/GroupNorm swaps (App.~\ref{app:norm_generalization}), the 5-family rescue sweep (Table~\ref{tab:rescue}), the vision cross-modality control (App.~\ref{app:vision_moe}), and the $(\mu,\sigma)$ re-injection failure (App.~\ref{app:reinjection}), these form the eight causal controls (Table~\ref{tab:causal_controls}, App.~\ref{app:causal_controls}); the only mechanism consistent with all eight is that \emph{instance normalization strips the per-window $(\mu,\sigma)$ signal the router needs}.
533	
